13
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:54,614
Let's begin.

14
00:00:55,733 --> 00:00:57,144
Our first world tonight.

15
00:00:58,266 --> 00:01:03,496
Thirty one light years from here is a small world called Gliese four three six b.

16
00:01:03,496 --> 00:01:11,014
About the size of Neptune, it circles its star so closely that one full year there lasts only two and a half days.

17
00:01:12,133 --> 00:01:17,448
Its surface runs near eight hundred degrees Fahrenheit. Hot enough to soften lead.

18
00:01:17,448 --> 00:01:28,486
And yet, astronomers believe much of this world is still, somehow, water ice — crushed into that shape not by cold, but by its own enormous gravity.

19
00:01:29,633 --> 00:01:40,082
It was found using the MEarth Project, a real network of small ground telescopes, built just to watch quiet red dwarf stars for the tiny flicker of a passing world.

20
00:01:41,199 --> 00:01:42,270
Our next world.

21
00:01:43,399 --> 00:01:48,659
TrES two b reflects less than one percent of the starlight that reaches it.

22
00:01:48,659 --> 00:01:51,288
Darker than coal. Darker than black paint.

23
00:01:52,433 --> 00:01:58,517
It was caught by NASA's Kepler space telescope, in a brightness dip of just six parts per million.

24
00:01:58,517 --> 00:02:03,587
At the time, the smallest signal ever measured from a planet beyond our solar system.

25
00:02:04,733 --> 00:02:10,314
Astronomers believe its dayside glows a dull, ember red, from its own trapped heat.

26
00:02:10,314 --> 00:02:13,902
No reflective clouds. Nothing to lighten it at all.

27
00:02:15,033 --> 00:02:16,104
Our next world.

28
00:02:17,233 --> 00:02:21,263
Fifty five Cancri e circles its star once every seventeen hours.

29
00:02:21,263 --> 00:02:23,461
Closer than Mercury circles our Sun.

30
00:02:23,461 --> 00:02:26,759
Its permanent dayside runs near twenty seven hundred kelvin.

31
00:02:26,759 --> 00:02:31,522
Hot enough to keep an entire hemisphere as standing lava, all the time.

32
00:02:32,666 --> 00:02:36,322
Astronomers once proposed its interior was one third diamond.

33
00:02:36,322 --> 00:02:42,008
A closer look at its star's own chemistry made that idea far less certain.

34
00:02:42,008 --> 00:02:45,257
A genuinely open question, not a settled fact.

35
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:56,926
It was studied with NASA's Spitzer Space Telescope, watching its temperature swing by hundreds of degrees, between one permanent day, and one permanent night.

36
00:02:58,066 --> 00:02:59,137
Our next world.

37
00:03:00,266 --> 00:03:07,598
HD one eight nine seven three three b is a cobalt blue gas giant, sixty four light years from here.

38
00:03:07,598 --> 00:03:11,264
Hubble measured that blue directly, from the planet's own light.

39
00:03:12,399 --> 00:03:14,088
That blue isn't an ocean.

40
00:03:14,088 --> 00:03:22,195
It's a sky full of silicate cloud, the same mineral family as glass, torn sideways by winds near fifty four hundred miles an hour.

41
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:28,871
Its own star is a flare prone dwarf, caught mid blast by astronomers.

42
00:03:28,871 --> 00:03:34,409
A real stellar flare, quietly stripping the planet's upper atmosphere away, into space.

43
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:37,767
If you're still here, thank you for staying.

44
00:03:37,767 --> 00:03:47,262
If you've ever wanted to be the one who lights up a room without saying a word — a Super Chat or a Gift right now does exactly that, right here on this screen.

45
00:03:48,399 --> 00:03:49,548
Our next world.

46
00:03:50,666 --> 00:03:55,542
WASP seventy six b runs above twenty four hundred degrees Celsius on its dayside.

47
00:03:55,542 --> 00:03:57,980
Hot enough to vaporize iron into gas.

48
00:03:59,099 --> 00:04:10,388
Supersonic winds drag that iron vapor toward the night side, where astronomers using the Very Large Telescope in Chile watched it quietly vanish from the gas phase, in twenty twenty.

49
00:04:10,388 --> 00:04:14,903
Exactly what you'd expect, if it were condensing back into solid metal.

50
00:04:16,033 --> 00:04:22,459
Their conclusion, published quietly in the journal Nature: on this planet, it rains iron.

51
00:04:23,599 --> 00:04:24,592
Our next world.

52
00:04:25,733 --> 00:04:32,316
WASP twelve b sits so close to its star that gravity has stretched it into an egg shape.

53
00:04:32,316 --> 00:04:38,899
Its star is slowly stripping its atmosphere away, atom by atom, into a glowing stream of stolen gas.

54
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:42,169
Its orbit is measurably shrinking.

55
00:04:42,169 --> 00:04:48,579
Current estimates give it roughly three million years, before it spirals into its star completely.

56
00:04:48,579 --> 00:04:51,997
Unusually soon, by the slow patience of astronomy.

57
00:04:53,133 --> 00:05:04,784
It was found using SuperWASP, a real network of quiet robotic telescopes in the Canary Islands and South Africa, built just to watch the whole sky dim, one tiny fraction at a time.

58
00:05:05,933 --> 00:05:07,004
Our next world.

59
00:05:08,133 --> 00:05:11,892
KELT nine b's dayside runs about forty six hundred kelvin.

60
00:05:11,892 --> 00:05:14,899
Hotter than most stars in this entire galaxy.

61
00:05:16,033 --> 00:05:22,067
At that heat, molecules simply can't survive. Hydrogen gas rips apart into loose atoms.

62
00:05:22,067 --> 00:05:28,964
Metals like iron and titanium drift free as bare vapor, never bonding into anything at all.

63
00:05:30,099 --> 00:05:34,421
It completes one full orbit in under thirty six hours.

64
00:05:34,421 --> 00:05:40,471
Close enough that its swollen atmosphere is slowly, quietly being carried away into space.

65
00:05:41,599 --> 00:05:46,575
Before our last world tonight — one planet you might expect isn't here.

66
00:05:46,575 --> 00:05:50,019
Tabby's Star, once linked to speculation about alien megastructures.

67
00:05:50,019 --> 00:05:54,611
Real follow up data points instead to orbiting dust and comet fragments.

68
00:05:54,611 --> 00:05:56,907
This list only counts what's confirmed.

69
00:05:58,033 --> 00:05:59,600
Our last world tonight.

70
00:06:00,733 --> 00:06:06,950
In nineteen ninety two, astronomers confirmed the very first planets ever found outside our solar system.

71
00:06:06,950 --> 00:06:10,059
They don't orbit a star like our Sun.

72
00:06:11,199 --> 00:06:18,151
They orbit a pulsar. The crushed, spinning corpse of a star that already exploded once.

73
00:06:18,151 --> 00:06:23,712
Astronomers nicknamed the pulsar Lich, and its worlds Draugr, Poltergeist, and Phobetor.

74
00:06:24,833 --> 00:06:29,841
The pulsar quietly fires X-rays and a wind of fast particles across all three worlds.

75
00:06:29,841 --> 00:06:35,517
Land on one, and the radiation would likely reach you before your boots ever touched the ground.

76
00:06:36,666 --> 00:06:44,246
The discovery came from the real, three hundred and five meter Arecibo radio telescope in Puerto Rico.

77
00:06:44,246 --> 00:06:49,597
Measuring anomalies in pulsar timing just milliseconds wide, patiently, over three years.

78
00:06:50,733 --> 00:06:54,239
Astronomers still don't fully know how these worlds formed.

79
00:06:54,239 --> 00:07:02,419
The leading idea is that they're second generation planets, quietly built from wreckage, after the star that made this pulsar died.

80
00:07:02,419 --> 00:07:03,977
Still an open question.

81
00:07:05,099 --> 00:07:08,963
If you're still with me — good. There's more.

82
00:07:08,963 --> 00:07:14,973
Three real signals, found by real telescopes, that scientists are honestly still puzzling over.

83
00:07:16,099 --> 00:07:18,130
It is nineteen seventy seven.

84
00:07:18,130 --> 00:07:24,223
In a field in Ohio, a radio telescope is listening to the sky, completely unattended.

85
00:07:25,366 --> 00:07:31,842
Days later, a volunteer named Jerry Ehman sits at his kitchen table with a stack of paper printouts.

86
00:07:31,842 --> 00:07:37,957
A few pages in, he stops. Six characters, in a column of numbers that should mean nothing.

87
00:07:39,099 --> 00:07:44,135
He circles them in red pen, and writes one quiet word in the margin.

88
00:07:44,135 --> 00:07:53,127
The exact sequence was six, E, Q, U, J, five — meaning the signal was roughly thirty times stronger than the background noise around it.

89
00:07:54,266 --> 00:08:06,525
It arrived almost exactly at the frequency hydrogen itself emits — the most common element in the universe, and the frequency every search for a signal was built to watch.

90
00:08:06,525 --> 00:08:11,428
It lasted seventy two seconds, rising and falling smoothly, like something real.

91
00:08:12,566 --> 00:08:16,425
It came from the direction of the constellation Sagittarius.

92
00:08:16,425 --> 00:08:22,428
And, quietly, it was never seen again. Not that night. Not the next night.

93
00:08:22,428 --> 00:08:25,000
Not in nearly fifty years since.

94
00:08:26,133 --> 00:08:35,036
Astronomers have pointed some of the most sensitive radio telescopes on Earth at that exact spot at least six separate times, across five decades.

95
00:08:35,036 --> 00:08:38,745
As recently as twenty twenty two, using two major observatories.

96
00:08:38,745 --> 00:08:40,971
Every search came back with nothing.

97
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:56,094
The most credible natural theory right now, published in twenty twenty four and twenty five, is that a violent flare from a dead, collapsed star briefly lit up a cold cloud of hydrogen gas, like a flashbulb.

98
00:08:56,094 --> 00:08:59,498
Even its own authors call it only a hypothesis.

99
00:09:00,633 --> 00:09:06,735
The telescope that heard it was quietly torn down in nineteen ninety eight, to make room for a golf course.

100
00:09:06,735 --> 00:09:08,261
It can never listen again.

101
00:09:09,399 --> 00:09:15,699
Nearly fifty years later, this is still, honestly, the strongest unexplained signal ever recorded.

102
00:09:15,699 --> 00:09:17,499
Not proof of anything.

103
00:09:17,499 --> 00:09:23,349
Just a real number, on a real printout, that nobody has ever explained.

104
00:09:24,466 --> 00:09:26,107
October nineteenth, twenty seventeen.

105
00:09:26,107 --> 00:09:32,668
On a mountain in Hawaii, a telescope built to watch for asteroids catches something moving wrong.

106
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:39,860
Astronomer Robert Weryk spots a faint streak. He checks the night before — it's there too.

107
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:43,648
Its path doesn't fit any normal orbit around our Sun.

108
00:09:43,648 --> 00:09:48,951
Within days, the math is undeniable: it came from outside our solar system entirely.

109
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:54,410
It's named 'Oumuamua — Hawaiian for scout, or messenger from afar.

110
00:09:54,410 --> 00:10:03,814
Its brightness swings by a factor of ten as it tumbles, meaning it's either wildly elongated, like a cigar, or flattened, like a pancake.

111
00:10:03,814 --> 00:10:07,732
Nothing in our own solar system is shaped like either.

112
00:10:08,866 --> 00:10:14,575
As it leaves, it speeds up slightly more than the Sun's gravity alone can explain.

113
00:10:14,575 --> 00:10:19,522
In a comet, that push comes from visible ice boiling off the surface.

114
00:10:19,522 --> 00:10:24,089
Telescopes look for exactly that. They find nothing. No tail. No gas.

115
00:10:24,089 --> 00:10:28,275
No dust — even passing closer to the Sun than Mercury.

116
00:10:29,399 --> 00:10:42,038
One Harvard astronomer, Avi Loeb, proposed it might be thin and light enough to be pushed by sunlight itself, like a sail — natural, or, his words, debris from someone else's technology.

117
00:10:42,038 --> 00:10:45,988
Almost no other astronomer in the field agrees with him.

118
00:10:47,133 --> 00:10:58,633
In twenty nineteen, fourteen of the world's leading comet and asteroid experts reviewed everything and concluded every observation is consistent with something entirely natural.

119
00:10:58,633 --> 00:11:03,904
Which kind of natural, though, is still quietly being argued about.

120
00:11:05,033 --> 00:11:07,862
Two more interstellar visitors have passed through since.

121
00:11:07,862 --> 00:11:09,984
Both behaved exactly like normal comets.

122
00:11:09,984 --> 00:11:18,826
Which makes the first one stand out even more — a confirmed visitor from another star system that did something we still can't fully explain.

123
00:11:19,966 --> 00:11:21,151
Two thousand fifteen.

124
00:11:21,151 --> 00:11:29,840
NASA's Kepler telescope has spent years quietly watching over a hundred thousand stars, for the tiny flicker of a planet passing by.

125
00:11:30,966 --> 00:11:32,738
One star stops everyone cold.

126
00:11:32,738 --> 00:11:37,343
Its brightness doesn't dip by the tiny, steady amount a planet would cause.

127
00:11:37,343 --> 00:11:47,972
It dips by up to twenty two percent, then climbs back, on no fixed schedule at all — where a planet the size of Jupiter blocks only about one percent.

128
00:11:49,099 --> 00:11:53,285
Astronomers call it Tabby's Star, after Yale astronomer Tabetha Boyajian.

129
00:11:53,285 --> 00:12:06,261
A year later, astronomer Jason Wright asked a genuinely serious question — his own words — could this be what an alien megastructure under construction would actually look like from Earth?

130
00:12:07,399 --> 00:12:10,148
Not the same as saying he found one.

131
00:12:10,148 --> 00:12:16,332
Other astronomers checked — comet swarms, dust rings, a star that may have swallowed a planet long ago.

132
00:12:16,332 --> 00:12:22,173
In twenty eighteen, a new dip was caught live, measured in multiple colors of light at once.

133
00:12:22,173 --> 00:12:27,670
It blocked blue light more than red — the signature of dust, not a solid object.

134
00:12:28,799 --> 00:12:32,444
Boyajian's own conclusion: dust is most likely the reason.

135
00:12:32,444 --> 00:12:35,683
Wright agreed the data ruled out alien megastructures.

136
00:12:35,683 --> 00:12:38,922
So — not aliens, that part is settled.

137
00:12:38,922 --> 00:12:45,805
What's still not settled is which kind of dust, from what source, and why it's this extreme.

138
00:12:46,933 --> 00:12:57,261
As recently as this year, one tentative, unconfirmed study suggests a hidden giant planet in the system, quietly stirring up comet debris.

139
00:12:57,261 --> 00:13:02,894
Genuinely bizarre. Mostly explained. Still, right now, missing its last few pieces.

140
00:13:04,033 --> 00:13:07,024
None of these three are proof of aliens.

141
00:13:07,024 --> 00:13:16,373
Every one of them, when scientists actually checked, pointed back toward something natural, something strange, or something we simply don't have an answer for yet.

142
00:13:16,373 --> 00:13:21,982
That's honestly the more interesting finding — the universe doesn't need aliens to be strange.

143
00:13:21,982 --> 00:13:24,226
It's strange enough on its own.

144
00:13:25,366 --> 00:13:26,499
That's everything for tonight.

145
